# Citation metadata, in the Citation File Format (https://citation-file-format.org).
# GitHub reads this file for its "Cite this repository" button.  Zenodo reads
# .zenodo.json instead, and ignores this file when both are present, so the
# two carry the same version, title, authors, license, keywords, and
# references; check_citation.py stops a build where they disagree.  The steps
# of a release are in README.md, under "Releasing".
#
# `doi:` is Zenodo's concept DOI, the one that always resolves to the newest
# release.  A release's own DOI does not exist until the release is archived,
# by which point this file is already inside it, so there is no `doi:` field
# until the release after the first archived one.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it as below.  Results computed with it
  come from mLOS and ShelterDataPrep, which ran unchanged inside the page, so
  also cite those two at the versions the page's footer reports.
title: "mLOSweb"
abstract: >-
  A web page that runs ShelterDataPrep and mLOS inside the browser, for animal
  shelters without an R or Python installation.  The user loads an export of
  the shelter's stays, as a CSV file or an Excel workbook, answers a few
  questions about it, and gets the mLOS plots, tables, workbook, and slide
  deck.  The data is not uploaded anywhere.  ShelterDataPrep runs in Pyodide
  (Python compiled to WebAssembly) and mLOS in webR (R compiled to
  WebAssembly).  The page runs a subset of mLOS: it leaves out the Weibull
  regression and the confidence bounds of the conditional outcome curves, and
  compares at most four periods, six intake groups, and six animal groups.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
    given-names: "Michael Loizos"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-11-10"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Shelter-Data-Analysis/mLOSweb"
url: "https://shelter-data-analysis.github.io/mLOSweb/"
keywords:
  - animal shelter
  - length of stay
  - survival analysis
  - WebAssembly
  - webR
  - Pyodide

# What the page runs and the demo exports it offers.  Software is cited by its
# concept DOI, with the version pins.py names in the notes; data by its
# version DOI, as the deposit that holds the exact bytes the build checks.
